{
  "summary": "The video captures the opening moments of an IELTS speaking test conducted at Rossiots Academy. The examiner, Maryam Shafi, formally introduces the test, the candidate Fernando Alonso Lopez (candidate number 0143278), and herself. The setting is a professional testing room with a table displaying the Canadian flag and the IELTS logo flag. The candidate sits opposite the examiner, listening attentively to the introduction.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate sits with his hands clasped tightly on the table and maintains a serious, neutral facial expression while the examiner speaks.",
      "goal": "Observe if this rigid posture and lack of smiling indicate high anxiety or a overly formal approach that might hinder natural conversation flow later in the test."
    },
    {
      "hint": "The candidate does not interrupt or make eye contact shifts until the examiner finishes her introduction.",
      "goal": "Assess his level of attentiveness and adherence to instructions, which are key components of the speaking assessment criteria."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 40–60 second window, the examiner thanks the candidate for providing identification, confirms receipt (\"Great. Thank you.\"), and then transitions into orienting him for the first part of the exam: a series of personal questions beginning with accommodation. Throughout this instructional passage, the candidate remains seated across the table, hands resting near the Canadian flag, with a steady, neutral expression and no visible interruption or verbal response. His posture and gaze direction appear consistent with the attentive, controlled demeanor noted in the earlier segments.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate does not nod, verbalize acknowledgment, or shift his gaze while the examiner explains the upcoming personal-question format; his hands remain clasped and his facial muscles show no micro-expression change even at the phrase \"personal questions\" and \"accommodation.\"",
      "goal": "Determine whether this stillness reflects genuine comfort and focused listening or a freeze-response masking rising anxiety now that substantive exam content is being introduced, by closer inspection of blink rate, jaw tension, and any subtle torso lean during the examiner's explanation."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 60–80 s window the candidate answers the house-vs-flat question. He states a clear preference for a house over a flat, explaining that a flat would force him to live around people and that he 'really don't like neighbours.' He adds that he likes spacious places, which is why houses suit him. Vocally, his delivery is halting: he opens with two 'um' fillers, inserts an 'uh' mid-sentence, and repeats 'um' before his final clause. His pacing is slow and uneven, with brief pauses between thoughts. Visually, he leans slightly forward, makes small open-palm gestures when describing the flat scenario, then settles back with hands resting near the table edge when talking about spaciousness. The examiner nods and utters a soft 'Mhm' twice, and the candidate briefly glances down before resuming eye contact. His facial expression remains neutral-to-mildly earnest throughout; no smile or frown accompanies the neighbour comment.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate uses three 'um/uh' fillers and two self-restarts while articulating a simple preference, yet his tone stays flat and unhurried rather than panicked; his hands make only minimal gestures and his gaze drops briefly before returning to the examiner.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the fillers reflect genuine spontaneous thought-processing (low cognitive load, comfortable pace) or a suppressed anxiety response masked by a deliberately calm exterior, by closer inspection of micro-pauses, breath patterns, and any shoulder or jaw tension during the 'I really don't like neighbours' line."
    },
    {
      "hint": "When stating he dislikes neighbours and prefers spacious places, the candidate's posture shifts from a slight forward lean (during the flat description) to a settled-back position with hands near the table edge, and his facial muscles show no smile or frown—only a neutral, matter-of-fact expression.",
      "goal": "Clarify whether the physical settling-back signals authentic comfort with solitude and personal space (a stable introverted preference) or a defensive withdrawal triggered by the topic of forced proximity, by examining whether the posture change coincides precisely with the word 'neighbours' or lags behind it, and whether any self-soothing hand movement (e.g., finger-tapping, sleeve-pulling) accompanies the shift."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "The candidate continues describing his living situation, stating he likes having his own bathroom and that his current house is 'pretty big,' reinforcing his earlier preference for spacious personal environments. When the examiner asks how long he has lived there, he answers 'five years so far,' layering in hedging markers ('like,' 'so far,' 'Um') and closing with 'as I said,' which references his prior statement as though seeking implicit confirmation. Visually, he remains seated with small, contained hand gestures near the table edge; the examiner responds with an encouraging 'Perfect' and writes notes while posing the follow-up. The overall exchange is calm and cooperative, with no visible tension or abrupt posture shifts, though the speech contains noticeable filler and a trailing-off quality at the segment's end.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate stacks hedging words ('like,' 'so far,' 'Um') and ends with 'as I said,' glancing down briefly before re-engaging the examiner, while his hands stay low and make only a single small open-palm gesture rather than the broader motions seen when he described the house size.",
      "goal": "Clarify whether the self-referencing ('as I said') and reduced gestural energy during the duration answer signal a mild need for external validation of narrative consistency (lowering self-assurance scores) or merely a natural conversational bridge, by comparing gesture amplitude and gaze-return speed here against his earlier, more expansive description of the house."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 100–120 s segment, the interviewee elaborates on his accommodation, stressing its size and layout: 'pretty big, spacious at the back,' with 'a big yard' and 'a separate place from my bedroom which is like crossing the yard.' His delivery is peppered with fillers ('um,' repeated 'like'), indicating he is constructing the spatial description in real time rather than reciting a rehearsed account. The interviewer responds with a brief acknowledgment ('Okay, great') and immediately pivots to a forward-looking question—'And do you plan to live there for a long time?'—shifting the exchange from descriptive to evaluative, probing commitment and future orientation.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The interviewee describes an unusual spatial relationship ('a separate place from my bedroom which is like crossing the yard') while stacking hedges ('like,' 'um'), and his hands appear to make a small sweeping or pointing gesture that does not clearly map onto the described layout.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the spatial description reflects genuine familiarity with the property (he is mentally walking through it, hence the gestural sweep) or psychological detachment / recent arrival (he is improvising a plausible-sounding layout, hence the hedging and imprecise gesture). Closer frame-by-frame inspection of hand trajectory relative to his gaze direction during 'crossing the yard' would clarify whether he is referencing a remembered space or fabricating one on the spot."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this segment (120–140 s), the interviewee responds to a question about future housing plans with layered uncertainty: 'I'm not really sure about it… I would like to have my own place at some point… I think I will be working right now more time… unless I have enough money to buy my own place.' He stacks four hedging expressions ('not really sure,' 'at some point,' 'I think,' 'unless') and two 'um' fillers while describing a plan that is contingent on financial capacity. Visually, he remains seated with hands on the table, maintaining a relatively still posture as he speaks. The examiner then closes the topic with 'Thank you very much' and pivots to 'Now let's talk about music,' signaling a structured transition to the next section of the interview.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate stacks four distinct hedging markers ('not really sure,' 'at some point,' 'I think,' 'unless') across a single short answer about housing, yet his vocal delivery stays flat and unhurried rather than showing rising pitch or increased speech rate that might signal anxiety; his hands remain largely stationary on the table throughout.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the concentration of hedges reflects a genuine life-stage ambiguity (a young adult realistically weighing financial constraints) or a habitual avoidance pattern in which the speaker defers commitment to any concrete future plan. Closer inspection of whether his gaze drops or drifts at each hedge boundary (e.g., right before 'at some point' and 'unless') versus staying fixed on the examiner would clarify whether the uncertainty is cognitive (genuinely thinking) or protective (avoiding a firm statement)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 20-second segment, the examiner asks whether the candidate likes listening to music. He opens with a drawn-out \"Mmm\" before affirming, \"I do love listening to music,\" using the emphatic auxiliary \"do.\" He then layers two fillers (\"Um,\" \"uh\") around the poetic claim that music \"basically cures your soul,\" delivered in the same flat, unhurried register heard throughout the interview. After the examiner's back-channel \"Mhm,\" he adds a structured routine—listening after work and before sleep—and specifies a preference for \"catchy tunes.\" Visually, his hands remain clasped on the table and his facial expression stays neutral; there is no smile, eyebrow raise, or gestural expansion that would typically accompany a statement about something one \"loves.\" The overall pattern mirrors earlier segments: substantive content is delivered with stacked hesitancy markers, minimal body movement, and a tonal flatness that contrasts with the emotional vocabulary he selects (\"love,\" \"cures your soul\").",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate says \"I do love listening to music\" and \"music basically cures your soul\"—high-emotion language—yet his vocal pitch stays level, his hands do not unclasp, and his face shows no micro-expression of warmth or excitement; the \"Mmm\" opener and two mid-sentence fillers further flatten the affective delivery.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the emotional vocabulary reflects a genuine internal feeling that is simply under-expressed externally (a stable low-expressivity trait) or a socially expected \"correct\" answer assembled from memorized phrases (\"cures your soul\" being a common idiom), by checking whether any brief pitch rise, breath change, or involuntary hand-release occurs on the word \"love\" or \"soul\" specifically, versus uniform flatness across the entire utterance."
    },
    {
      "hint": "He structures his answer around two fixed time slots (\"after work and before I go to sleep\") and a narrow genre qualifier (\"catchy tunes\"), giving the response a checklist-like completeness despite the surrounding fillers.",
      "goal": "Clarify whether this tidy enumeration signals a genuinely routine-bound listening habit (the candidate has a real daily ritual) or a rehearsed template designed to sound thorough under assessment pressure, by observing whether his gaze drifts upward or to the side (memory retrieval) versus staying locked on the examiner (recitation) during the time-slot listing."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "When asked how much time he spends listening to music daily, the candidate opens with a prolonged 'Hmm' and admits he hasn't thought about it until now, accompanied by a brief self-deprecating chuckle. He then offers a heavily hedged estimate—'I guess that I listen to around like two, three hours of music a day'—stacking three qualifiers ('I guess,' 'around,' 'like') before landing on a range rather than a number. He adds contextual detail ('especially when I'm working') and begins a contrasting clause ('but I try not to work with…') that is cut off mid-thought. His vocal delivery is unhurried and flat, with no rise in pitch or energy despite discussing a personally meaningful habit; his hands remain largely stationary on the table, and his gaze shifts briefly downward during the hedging sequence before returning to the examiner. The overall impression is of someone genuinely reconstructing an answer in real time rather than reciting a prepared script, yet also someone who avoids committing to precise self-assessment.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate explicitly states 'I haven't thought about that until now' with a light chuckle, then immediately contradicts this by producing a structured time estimate and a work-context qualifier within the same breath—suggesting the 'haven't thought about it' framing may be a social softener rather than a true admission of unfamiliarity with his own habit.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the initial 'I haven't thought about that' + chuckle functions as a genuine moment of introspective surprise (indicating low self-monitoring and authentic spontaneity) or as a rehearsed humility device that preempts judgment for an imprecise answer. Closer inspection of micro-expression timing—specifically whether the chuckle precedes or follows the verbal admission, and whether his eyebrows lift in a true 'realization' pattern versus a polite social smile—would clarify whether the surprise is felt or performed."
    },
    {
      "hint": "The candidate's hedging density here ('I guess,' 'around like,' 'two, three hours') is markedly higher than in the preceding 140–160 s segment where he listed fixed time-slots ('after work and before I go to sleep') with checklist-like confidence, despite both segments addressing the same music-listening topic.",
      "goal": "Clarify whether the shift from structured enumeration (previous segment) to vague quantification (this segment) reflects a genuine distinction between knowing WHEN he listens (routine awareness) versus HOW MUCH (duration awareness), or whether it reveals a pattern of avoiding numerical self-commitment whenever the question demands a measurable metric. Comparing his gaze stability, filler frequency, and hand-gesture amplitude across both windows would indicate whether the vagueness is topic-specific or a broader avoidance of quantified self-disclosure."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In this 180–200 s window, the candidate articulates a sensory-control rationale for his music preferences: he favors songs he can sing along to because non-singable tracks 'distract' him, and he rejects live music because the audible presence of other attendees singing or attempting to sing disrupts his listening experience. His delivery features a deliberate pause and the qualifier 'Honestly' before the rejection, followed by a trailing-off explanation ('singing or trying to…'), indicating real-time thought formulation rather than recitation. Visually, he maintains his established clasped-hands posture, neutral facial expression, and steady gaze toward the examiner, with no notable gestural expansion or micro-expression shift during either the preference statement or the rejection of live music.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate's justification for disliking live music centers on hearing 'all the people singing or trying to'—a sensory-overload framing—while his earlier stated dislike of neighbours and preference for 'spacious places' used a spatial-proximity framing. Both responses share a common thread of other-people-as-disruption, yet the candidate never explicitly links them.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the music-preference reasoning is an independent sensory observation or an unconscious extension of the broader environmental-sensitivity pattern established in the neighbours/spaciousness segment. Closer inspection of whether his vocal tone, filler usage, and hand-position micro-shifts during the live-music rejection mirror those during the neighbours answer (indicating a shared emotional register) versus diverge (indicating genuinely separate topics) would clarify whether this is a unified personality trait manifesting across contexts or two unrelated preferences."
    },
    {
      "hint": "The candidate inserts 'Honestly' with a noticeable pause ('um') before stating he does not like live music, then trails off mid-explanation ('singing or trying to…') without completing the thought, while maintaining his flat vocal pitch and clasped posture.",
      "goal": "Clarify whether the 'Honestly' qualifier signals awareness that his answer deviates from a socially expected enthusiastic response (implying mild social-monitoring beneath the flat affect) or simply functions as a discourse marker for emphasis. The incomplete trailing-off could indicate either that he lost his train of thought under assessment pressure or that he deliberately stopped before over-explaining. Comparing the pause length before 'Honestly' against his earlier pauses before 'I do love listening to music' (where he used high-emotion language without hedging) would reveal whether the live-music rejection carries a different cognitive load."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "The candidate affirms loving traveling with an echoing structure ('I do love traveling' mirroring the examiner's 'Do you like traveling?'), then stacks three generic benefits—knowing new places, meeting new people, making new relationships—separated by 'um' fillers. He attempts a concrete illustration ('I imagine that I can have a friend in another country that…') but trails off mid-sentence without completing the thought, preceded by a restart ('that's like just uh'). His posture remains hands-clasped and facial expression neutral throughout, consistent with the flat affect observed in earlier segments. Notably, the claimed enthusiasm for 'meeting new people' and 'making new relationships' stands in direct tension with his earlier stated dislike of neighbours, preference for spacious solitude, and aversion to live-music crowds ('all the people singing or trying to').",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate lists 'meeting new people, making new relationships' as travel joys with two 'um' fillers and a restart, then begins a specific example ('I imagine that I can have a friend in another country that…') but abandons it mid-clause without finishing the thought, while his hands stay clasped and his gaze does not widen or brighten as one would expect if the social enthusiasm were genuinely felt.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the stated love of 'meeting new people' reflects authentic extroverted curiosity or a socially expected answer performed under assessment pressure, by closely inspecting whether the trailing-off coincides with a micro-expression shift (e.g., slight brow furrow, lip press, or downward gaze drift) that would signal discomfort with the interpersonal claim, and by comparing the filler density and restart pattern here against his earlier, more fluent descriptions of solitary preferences (the house, the yard, the bedroom)."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "In response to a future-travel question, the candidate names two specific countries (Italy and Japan) and supplies a concrete rationale (Asian cuisine), yet frames the entire answer with hedges ('at some point,' 'actually'), fillers ('Um,' 'Uh'), and a self-restart ('in my, in my city'). His vocal delivery stays flat and measured despite the positive vocabulary ('love,' 'would love to visit'), consistent with his affective pattern across the interview. Notably, he grounds an abstract future aspiration in a present sensory anchor (Asian food already available in his city), suggesting concrete rather than imaginative orientation. This segment stands out as more structurally complete than his earlier trailing-off answers (the abandoned 'friend in another country that…' clause, the fractured 'makes me feel'), raising the question of whether the specificity reflects genuine enthusiasm or simply a formula-friendly prompt.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate provides two named countries and a stated reason (cuisine) with noticeably less trailing-off than his 220–260 s answers, yet still wraps the response in 'at some point' and 'actually,' delivers 'love' in the same flat pitch used for neutral statements, and self-restarts only once ('in my, in my city') before completing the thought cleanly.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the increased structural completeness here reflects genuine affective engagement with the travel topic (he has actually thought about these destinations and feels warmth toward the food connection) or is simply the easiest question format to answer with a memorized list, by closely inspecting whether his gaze lifts or his eyebrows raise even briefly when he says 'Japan' and 'cuisine' versus remaining locked in the same neutral, slightly downward stare he held during the flat 'not really sure' housing answer at 120–140 s. If the eyes stay fixed and the shoulders do not shift, the specificity is likely cognitive retrieval rather than emotional recall."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "summary": "The candidate closes Part 1 with a concise, well-formed preference statement—\"Um, I like trying that kind of food, so I would like to try the original one\"—preceded by a single filler but containing a clear causal connector and no restarts or trailing-offs. This is markedly cleaner than his earlier answers, which were dense with hedges (\"like,\" \"um,\" \"at some point\"), self-corrections, and incomplete clauses. The examiner then formally announces the end of Part 1 (\"Now we are done with the first part of the exam\"), marking a structural transition. Visually, the candidate remains seated with hands near the table, maintaining the same neutral, slightly reserved posture he has held throughout, while the examiner gestures toward her notes to signal closure.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hint": "The candidate's final Part 1 answer is structurally complete (subject–verb–object + causal clause) with only one opener filler, contrasting sharply with the 4–5 hedges and mid-sentence abandonments seen in his 120–260 s responses; simultaneously, his body language during the examiner's wrap-up shows no visible shoulder-drop, exhale, or hand-unclasping that would signal relief at the section ending.",
      "goal": "Determine whether the sudden cleanliness of this last answer reflects genuine topic ease (food preference is low-stakes and concrete, so he can retrieve a ready-made opinion without hedging) or assessment fatigue / disengagement (he has stopped investing effort in elaboration because Part 1 is nearly over). Closer frame-by-frame inspection of whether his gaze lifts toward the examiner during the wrap-up announcement (active listening / readiness for Part 2) versus drifts downward or to the side (mental shutdown / relief that the section is ending) would distinguish cognitive ease from motivational withdrawal."
    }
  ]
}
